// EXPECT: currently-rejected
// Violates: docs/OWNERSHIP.md R7's consumption clause (an arm binding may not
// be consumed while the scrutinee still owns the value).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This was the largest live memory-safety defect left in
// the language, and it is the sibling of
// `examples/rejected/owned_move_through_match.cell` next to it: that one is a
// `match` in an `owned` SLOT, this one is a `match` BINDING in an `owned`
// slot. They look alike and fail for opposite reasons. There, `placeOf`
// returned null and the site READ a value it should have refused. Here
// `placeOf` returns a perfectly good place, the site MOVED it, and the move
// was of the wrong thing: R7 does not move the scrutinee, so the arm binding
// is an ALIAS of it and not a second owner.
//
// Measured end to end against `zig-out/bin/cell` built at `4698dbc`, with
// `make`, `take` and `print_int` supplied by a C host whose `take` frees:
//
//     cell check                                    exit 0
//     cc -fsanitize=address                         exit 0
//     running it                                    exit 134
//
//     AddressSanitizer: attempting double-free
//       free <- cell_string_free <- cell_main
//
// The emitted C was:
//
//     cell_string_t s1 = cell_make();
//     cell_string_t _cell_t1 = s1;
//     cell_string_t x = _cell_t1;      /* the arm binding COPIES the header */
//     _cell_t0 = cell_take(x);         /* the callee frees the buffer */
//     ...
//     cell_string_free(&s1);           /* and so does the scope drop */
//
// ELEVEN SHAPES WERE LIVE, not the one the brief named. The brief's matrix had
// five rows and was measured by its author; the enumeration below is what
// forty programs said. Every one of these was `cell check` exit 0 and running
// it exit 134 at `4698dbc`:
//
//     print_int(match s1 { x => take(owned x) })            call argument
//     match seed { x => { return x } }                      return
//     match s1 { x => { d = x } }                           assignment
//     var owned d = make(); match d { x => take(owned x) }  var owned source
//     match xs { x => take_list(owned x) }  over [Int]      non-String type
//     fn f(shared xs: [Int]) { match xs { x => ... } }      BORROWED source
//     match s1 { x => match x { y => take(owned y) } }      nested one deep
//     match s1 { "yes" => 1, x => take(owned x) }           multi-arm
//     while i < 1 { match s1 { x => take(owned x) } }       inside a loop
//     match a { x => take_list(owned x) }  over arc [Int]   R10 LAUNDERED
//     match a { x => match x { y => ... } } over arc [Int]  both at once
//
// The last row is worth its own sentence. `take_list(owned a)` on an
// `arc [Int]` place is refused by R10 and has been for some time. Binding the
// same place through a `match` arm defeated that refusal completely, because
// `arcUniqueSource` reads the ARM BINDING's own annotation and `checkMatch`
// declares every arm binding `.owned`. One hole, two rules escaping through
// it.
//
// The `shared [Int]` row is worth a second one. The `String` spelling of that
// same program ran CLEAN, because `owned String` and `shared String` are
// different C types and codegen inserted a `cell_string_from_str` copy, while
// `owned [T]` and `shared [T]` are the same type and it inserted nothing. A
// rule whose enforcement depends on which two C types happen to coincide is
// exactly what `refuseArcUnique`'s comment already refuses to write, so both
// spellings are refused.
//
// WHY IT IS REFUSED AND NOT MOVED. Moving the scrutinee is the better
// semantics and is recorded as the designed follow-up in
// `docs/OWNERSHIP.md` R7. It would additionally make a later `take(owned s1)`
// report use-after-move for the right reason, which is currently accepted
// because the move never happened. It is not done here: it changes `wasMoved`
// for every arm binding and `codegen.zig`'s `pendingDrops` reads exactly that,
// so it reschedules drops. Three separate fixes in this repository have
// shipped a NEW silent miscompile in the fixer's own first commit, every one
// of them by changing what a binding holds or when it is dropped without
// asking what reads that.
//
// HOW IT IS ENFORCED, which is the reusable part. Not by a list of shapes.
// ONE question, `armAlias`, asked at the top of `ownedMoveSource` above its
// `.place` return, so every `owned` consumption site inherits the answer and a
// site added later inherits it without knowing R7 exists. Its verdict is a new
// `.aliases_place` variant, and adding that variant is what enumerated the
// sites: those switches have no `else` arm, so the compiler refused to build
// until each one answered.
//
// WHAT STILL COMPILES, so "refused" is scoped to mean something. The condition
// is "does anything else still own this", not "is this an arm binding":
//
//     match make() { x => take(owned x) }                   no other owner
//     match s1 { x => use_it(shared x) }                    a read, not owned
//     match n { x => show(copy x) }                         copy duplicates
//     match s1 { x => Tag { name: x } }                     see below
//
// The last one READS, exactly as `Tag { name: s1 }` already does. Those two
// are the same latent hazard, R11's record drop, and neither is a double free
// today; splitting one gap across two rules would buy nothing.
//
// THE NAMED OVER-REFUSALS, all in the safe direction and all measured exit 0
// before this rule: a struct FIELD as scrutinee, an `owned` parameter as
// scrutinee, a `shared String` parameter or borrow as scrutinee, a field of an
// aliasing arm binding, and `let owned y = x` inside an arm. Each is a latent
// double free held open only by a disclosed R11 gap (a record is never
// dropped, a slice element is never released, an `owned` parameter is never
// dropped, an arm binding is never dropped). The file's standing choice is to
// refuse a latent case alongside the live ones rather than leave it as a trap
// for the change that lands those drops.
//
// A SIXTH over-refusal, and it is the interesting one because the first
// version of this rule ACCEPTED it:
//
//     match fresh() { x => match x { y => take(owned y) } }
//
// is safe today and refused anyway. That version propagated "no other owner"
// through a whole arm binding, which is sound about ONE consumer and false
// about two:
//
//     match fresh() { x => match x { y => take(owned y) } + take(owned x) }
//
// was exit 134 under it. `x` and `y` are two different bindings holding one
// buffer, so R2 cannot see it, and nesting a `match` is the ONLY construct in
// this grammar that makes two live handles on one value: `let owned y = x`
// MOVES `x`, which is why the `let` spelling was already safe. The precision
// was worth one contrived program and cost a live double free.
//
// WHAT IS STILL LIVE, so "the last defect" is not claimed here. Found while
// measuring this one and NOT fixed by it, because it is R2.b's rule and R11's
// gap rather than R7's:
//
//     let owned s1 = make()
//     let owned t: Tag = Tag { name: s1 }
//     take(owned t.name)                     exit 134, at 2b05a2a and after
//
// R2.b leaves a plain place a READ at the struct-field position, arguing that a
// record is never dropped so the buffer is freed once by `s1`. That holds only
// while nobody moves the field back OUT, and `t.name` is a different place from
// `s1`, so R2 sees no use-after-move and both are freed.

pub fn make() -> String;
pub fn take(owned s: String) -> Int;
pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    let owned s1: String = make()
    print_int(match s1 { x => take(owned x) })
}
